From 603cef3a33de50fde51f415bfb99c4ad2b1393cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:26:45 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: hook the arm64 architecture

Everything the series changes in arm64's own code, as the x86 hook patch
does for x86-64, and arm64's per-architecture recorder files.

Hooked kernel files:
- arch/arm64/kernel/syscall.c calls the recorder around invoke_syscall()
  in el0_svc_common(), the one C path every AArch64 and AArch32 call
  takes, after seccomp and a tracer had their say (the call number is
  the one that runs; a call they skip is not recorded, as on x86).
- arch/arm64/kernel/setup.c takes the antinomie_* options out of the
  command line right after the device tree (or the EFI stub's) supplied
  it, before the early options are parsed and before the kernel prints
  it; reserves the bootstrap page from memblock right after
  arm64_memblock_init(), before the first allocation; and, on a device
  tree boot, gives /chosen/bootargs the command line the kernel kept,
  since /proc/device-tree shows it to every user.
- arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c takes antinomie_kpti=, kpti= under
  another name: the host's "faster emulation" choice, which /proc/cmdline
  then does not show.
- arch/arm64/Kconfig selects HAVE_ANTINOMIE.

Recorder files (kernel/antinomie):
- capture_arm64.c decodes the frame rt_sigreturn reads (siginfo and
  ucontext, and the extra space an extra_context record points to) and
  the MTE tag requests of ptrace (PEEKMTETAGS, POKEMTETAGS).
- debug_arm64.c names the architecture in the debug CAPS and offers no
  debugging yet: no task is one the engine debugs, so ATTACH answers
  EOPNOTSUPP.

No arm64 hook reports a single step or a breakpoint yet, and AArch32
calls are recorded with their number and no table.

Contract: unchanged (revision 20).
